NAME
       audiotest - play test signals and measure audio latency

SYNOPSIS
       audiotest [MODE] [-f HZ] [-F HZ] [-a LEVEL] [-t SECONDS] [-c CHANNEL]
                 [-n CHANNELS] [-r RATE] [-L ROUNDS] [-v VOLUME] [-b MS]
                 [--linear] [--s16] [-q]

DESCRIPTION
       audiotest generates the signals that answer the usual questions about a
       sound setup -- does anything come out, is left left, are  the  surround
       speakers in the right places, does the bass reach the  subwoofer,  what
       does the room do to a sweep, how long does sound take to  come  out  --
       and plays them through the audio service,  audiod(1),  like  any  other
       program.

       The signal is synthesised in floating point and sent as  32-bit  float,
       or as 16-bit with triangular  dither  with  --s16.  Signals  of  finite
       length fade in and out over 15 ms so they  start  and  stop  without  a
       click. The default level is -12 dBFS: clearly audible, never painful at
       a sensible master volume.

MODES
       The mode is the first word on the command line or the argument of -m.

       sine   A sine tone, 440 Hz unless -f says  otherwise,  for  5  seconds.
              Also tone.

       channels
              Speaker identification. Each  channel  in  turn  makes  a  short
              voice-like call of its own while its name is printed:  the  left
              speakers a falling melody, the right  ones  a  rising  one,  the
              centre a level one, and the front, side and rear positions  each
              a different vowel and register, so a swapped pair  is  heard  as
              wrong without looking at the screen. The subwoofer channel (LFE)
              gives two low thumps instead. The channel order is the usual one
              for the count: mono; FL FR; FL FR FC; FL FR BL BR; FL FR  FC  BL
              BR; FL FR FC LFE BL BR (5.1); FL FR FC LFE BC SL SR (6.1); FL FR
              FC LFE BL BR SL SR (7.1). Each name is printed when its call  is
              actually heard. Also speakers.

       sweep  A sine sweep from 20 Hz (or -f) to 20 kHz (or -F, at  most  just
              under half the rate), logarithmic so each octave takes the  same
              time, or linear  with  --linear;  10  seconds  by  default.  The
              current frequency is shown. Useful for finding  the  lowest  and
              highest tones a speaker reproduces and for rattles.

       white  White noise: equal energy per hertz. Also noise.

       pink   Pink noise: equal energy per octave, which sounds  even  to  the
              ear; the usual signal for setting speaker  levels  against  each
              other.

       square, saw, triangle
              The classic waveforms at -f, band-limited so they do  not  alias
              at high frequencies.

       silence
              Digital silence: keeps a stream open, for example to check  what
              an idle stream costs.

       latency
              Measures instead of testing the ear. For -t seconds (default  5)
              a quiet 1 kHz tone plays while the latency the  service  reports
              is sampled every 50  ms  and  the  frames  actually  played  are
              compared with the system clock. Then a 10 ms  click  is  written
              into an empty stream  several  times  and  the  time  until  the
              service reports it heard  is  measured.  The  result  shows  the
              reported latency (minimum, average, maximum), the write-to-heard
              time, and the rate the device really plays at with its deviation
              from the nominal rate in parts per million; more than  0.2%  off
              is flagged. With -b the effect of the stream's buffering can  be
              compared.

OPTIONS
       -m, --mode MODE
              The signal, as above.

       -f, --freq HZ
              Frequency of sine, square, saw and triangle  (default  440),  or
              the start of a sweep (default 20). 1k is 1000.

       -F, --to HZ
              End frequency of a sweep (default 20000).

       --linear
              Sweep linearly in frequency.

       -a, --amplitude LEVEL
              Peak level: a fraction 0..1,  a  percentage  (50%)  or  decibels
              below full scale (-6dB). Default -12 dB.

       -t, --time SECONDS
              Duration; fractions allowed; 0 plays until  Ctrl-C  (not  for  a
              sweep). Defaults: 5 seconds, 10 for a sweep. The  channels  mode
              counts rounds instead (-L).

       -c, --channel CHANNEL
              Play only on one channel, the others silent: a number from 1, or
              a position name of the layout (FL, FR, FC, LFE, BL, BR, SL,  SR,
              BC), or left or right.  In  channels  mode,  only  that  channel
              calls.

       -n, --channels N
              Channels of the stream, 1..8 (default 2). The service maps  them
              to the speakers that exist.

       -r, --rate HZ
              Sample rate of the stream, 8000..384000 (default 48000).

       -L, --rounds N
              Channels mode: how many times to go round all  speakers;  0  for
              ever. Default 1.

       -v, --volume N
              The stream's volume, 0..100.

       -b, --buffer MS
              Buffering asked of the service, 1..10000 ms.

       --s16  Send 16-bit samples with dither instead of float.

       -q, --quiet
              No messages (the latency results are still printed).

       -h, --help
              A summary of the options.

EXAMPLES
       audiotest                          440 Hz for 5 seconds
       audiotest channels                 which speaker is which
       audiotest channels -n 6 -L 0       5.1 setup, round and round
       audiotest pink -c FR -t 0          level one speaker by ear
       audiotest sine -f 50 -c LFE -n 6   is the subwoofer connected?
       audiotest sweep -t 20 -a -20dB     slow, quiet sweep
       audiotest latency -b 20            how low can the latency go?

EXIT STATUS
       0      The signal played (or the measurement finished).

       1      The audio service could not be reached or failed.

       2      A bad option.

       130    Interrupted by Ctrl-C; the buffered sound is dropped.

SEE ALSO
       audioctl(1), audioplay(1), audioinfo(1), audiod(1)

